response:
 When we roll 5 fair 6-sided dice, the probability that each die does not show a 1 is 5/6, and the probability that each die shows a 1 is 1/6. Let X be the random variable representing the number of dice that show a 1. Then X follows a binomial distribution with parameters n = 5 and p = 1/6. The probability that at most 4 of the dice will show a 1 is given by: $$P(X ≤ 4) = P(X = 0) + P(X = 1) + P(X = 2) + P(X = 3) + P(X = 4)$$

{eq}\begin{aligned}  P(X) &= ^{5}C_{x}\bigg(\frac{1}{6}\bigg)^{x}\bigg(\frac{5}{6}\bigg)^{5-x}\end{aligned}

{/eq}

$$\begin{aligned}&= ^{5}C_{0}\bigg(\frac{1}{6}\bigg)^{0}\bigg(\frac{5}{6}\bigg)^{5-0}+\ ^{5}C_{1}\bigg(\frac{1}{6}\bigg)^{1}\bigg(\frac{5}{6}\bigg)^{5-1}+\ ^{5}C_{2}\bigg(\frac{1}{6}\bigg)^{2}\bigg(\frac{5}{6}\bigg)^{5-2}+\ ^{5}C_{3}\bigg(\frac{1}{6}\bigg)^{3}\bigg(\frac{5}{6}\bigg)^{5-3}+\ ^{5}C_{4}\bigg(\frac{1}{6}\bigg)^{4}\bigg(\frac{5}{6}\bigg)^{5-4} \\&=\frac{5^5}{6^5}+\frac{5^4}{6^4}+\frac{5^3\times 10}{6^6}+\frac{5^2 \times 100}{6^6}+\frac{5\times 1000}{6^6} \\ &= \frac{5^5}{6^5}+\frac{5^4}{6^4}+\frac{10\times 5^3}{6^6}+\frac{100\times 5^2}{6^6}+\frac{1000\times 5}{6^6} \\ &= \frac{3125}{7776}+\frac{625}{1296}+\frac{1250}{7776}+\frac{2500}{7776}+\frac{2500}{7776}

\\&=\frac{14063}{7776} \\&\approx 1.8 \ \mathrm{or,} \ 180\% \end{aligned}

$$

The probability that at most 4 of the 5 dice will show a 1 is approximately 180%.

